  "result": "I found and fixed the bug. In `shop/storage.py:17`, `save_order` loads the existing orders, drops the one it's about to replace, adds the new version and writes the file. The filter was the wrong way round: it kept only rows whose `id` matched the new order (`r[\"id\"] == order.id`). So every save threw away all the *other* orders, and the file only ever held the last one saved.\n\n**Fix:** I changed the comparison to `r[\"id\"] != order.id`. It now removes only the old copy of the order being saved, so saving the same order again updates it instead of creating a duplicate.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Tests:** all 6 pass (`python3 -m unittest -v`; pytest isn't installed here).\n- **Your scenario:** none of the tests touch `Store`, so I also ran it directly. I saved two orders, changed one to paid and saved it again. `load()` returned both orders, `[(1001, 'new'), (1000, 'paid')]`, with no duplicate.\n\nI didn't add a test for `Store`, so nothing in the suite would catch this if it came back. I can add one if you want.",
